::GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation changes size but preserves shape? {
=A. Dilation#A dilation scales lengths by a factor while preserving angle measures, so it supports similarity.
~B. Translation#A translation moves a figure without changing size.
~C. Reflection#A reflection flips a figure without changing size.
~D. Rotation#A rotation turns a figure without changing size.
}

::GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.3<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence says rotate, then dilate. What happens first? {
~A. The dilation#This reverses the stated sequence.
=B. The rotation#Transformation sequences are read in order unless notation states otherwise.
~C. Both at the same time#The sequence describes ordered steps.
~D. Neither transformation#Both transformations are part of the sequence.
}

::GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure maps onto itself after a 180&deg; turn. What symmetry is shown? {
~A. Reflection symmetry#Reflection symmetry uses a mirror line, not a turn.
~B. Translation symmetry#Translation symmetry slides a repeated figure.
=C. Rotation symmetry#Mapping onto itself after a turn is rotation symmetry.
~D. No symmetry#The figure maps onto itself, so symmetry is present.
}

::GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.3<br><br>Question:<br>Two triangles have two pairs of congruent corresponding angles. Which criterion justifies similarity? {
~A. SSS congruence#The evidence is angle equality for similarity, not equal side lengths for congruence.
~B. HL congruence#HL applies to right-triangle congruence.
~C. CPCTC#CPCTC is used after congruence is proven.
=D. AA similarity#Two congruent angle pairs are enough to establish triangle similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L08_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.1<br><br>Question:<br>Similar figures have corresponding sides 4 and 10. What is the scale factor from the first to the second? {
=A. 2.5#The scale factor is 10 divided by 4, which equals 2.5.
~B. 6#Subtracting side lengths does not give scale factor.
~C. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor.
~D. 14#Adding side lengths does not describe similarity.
}
